.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt HTTPS-DOCUMENT 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm https-document
.Nd fetch one configured authenticated document under an explicit grant
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl Fl sample
.Nm
.Fl Fl boot Ar hex
.Fl Fl pit-ms Ar milliseconds
.Fl Fl rtc Ar seconds
.Fl Fl utc-ms Ar milliseconds
.Fl Fl uncertainty Ar milliseconds
.Fl Fl grant-expiry Ar milliseconds
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This optional App A command is installed only when the operator composition
enables it.
It is a bounded document transaction, not a general URL client.
The operator supplies a time attestation and one finite grant; the program
uses its supplied stream authority and filesystem binding.
It refuses missing stream authority and unexpected NIC or block authority.
.Pp
.Fl Fl sample
prints a boot id, monotonic PIT milliseconds and RTC seconds to standard
error in an
.Ql absd-https-sample/1
record.
It does not establish trusted UTC or issue a grant.
.Pp
The fetch form requires all six distinct option/value pairs, in any order.
Boot is 16 bytes encoded as 32 lowercase hexadecimal digits.
The other fields use canonical unsigned decimal, without leading zeroes.
The attestation relates boot-local PIT and RTC observations to UTC with an
uncertainty bound.
Grant expiry is boot-local milliseconds, strictly after invocation and no
more than 15000 ms ahead.
Stale, inconsistent or unavailable time is refused; command-line values
are not independently authenticated by this parser.
Use the established App A operator procedure to supply them.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Pa /etc/https-document.conf
is UTF-8, at most 2048 bytes, exactly four lines in this order:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
peer=IPv4:port
name=lowercase.dns.name
path=/request-target
issuer_spki=64-lowercase-hex-digits
.Ed
.Pp
The peer is canonical IPv4 with a nonzero port, not a DNS lookup request.
The DNS name is checked separately against the certificate.
The request target begins with a slash, has at most 1024 printable nonspace
ASCII bytes and contains no fragment marker.
.Pa /etc/https-issuer.der
holds the nonempty issuer DER, at most 4096 bytes.
The configured SPKI pin, issuer, leaf name and verified time interval must
all satisfy the direct-issuer policy.
No HTTP application request is sent before authentication.
.Sh OUTPUT AND DURABILITY
A successful transaction publishes a new directory under
.Pa /home/operator/https-document- Ns Ar nonce
containing
.Pa body
and
.Pa receipt .
The body is bounded to 16384 bytes; the receipt records peer, name, path,
issuer pin, boot, verification/publication time intervals, byte count and
SHA-256.
Each file is created exclusively, written and synced in a new staging
directory; time and grant are checked again before a no-replace rename
publishes the directory.
The final receipt path is printed on standard error.
No response body is printed on standard output.
.Pp
Transaction failures may emit a bounded
.Ql absd-https-failure/1
record followed by refusal text on standard error.
Local stream acceptance or queue drain is not proof of peer receipt.
If publication outcome is refused or unknown, the refusal does not report
the generated staging or final path.
Preserve any corresponding artifacts under
.Pa /home/operator
for operator reconciliation; do not blindly retry or remove guessed artifacts.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 for a successful sample or publication; 1 for refusal or failure,
including malformed arguments.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Sample only, without initiating a fetch:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
https-document --sample
.Ed
.Pp
A fetch requires the configured peer/issuer, an operator time attestation
and the original stream grant; copying sample values alone is insufficient.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sync 1 ,
.Xr filesystem 7 ,
.Xr handles 7 ,
.Xr process 7
.Pp
The matching platform source's App A policy and operator documents define
the supported TLS, HTTP, time and authority envelope; this page grants
no wider network or certificate compatibility.
